{
  "case_id": "2007-06-1287",
  "document_hash": "bc27c1219c7a7059458e5d941385c3e875ad97095f66cd3513c307675135e5fe",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1287",
      "confidence": 0.96,
      "document_hash": "bc27c1219c7a7059458e5d941385c3e875ad97095f66cd3513c307675135e5fe",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bc27c1219c7a-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:04 a.m.\nAPPEARANCES:\nCARTER G. PHILLIPS, ESQ., Washington, D.C.; on behalf of the Petitioner.\nDOUGLAS HALLWARD-DRIEMEIER, ESQ., Assistant to the Solicitor General, Department of Justice, Washington, D.C.; on behalf of the United States, as amicus curiae, supporting the Petitioner.\nWARREN R. CALVERT, ESQ., Senior Assistant Attorney General, Atlanta, Ga.; on behalf of the Respondents."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Carter G. Phillips",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1287",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "bc27c1219c7a7059458e5d941385c3e875ad97095f66cd3513c307675135e5fe",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bc27c1219c7a-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Congress in what is now Section 11501(b)(1) crafted what this Court described as a straightforward approach to determining whether a State is unlawfully taxing railroad property. Congress used a precise formula that is set out in a pictorial form on page 6 of the Petitioner's brief, and I think it's probably easier to kind of follow along on that."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1287",
      "confidence": 0.94,
      "document_hash": "bc27c1219c7a7059458e5d941385c3e875ad97095f66cd3513c307675135e5fe",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bc27c1219c7a-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Is it realistic to think that you can calculate with any degree of precision what the true market value of CSX's property is? The range of values that were calculated by both of these experts are really astonishing, from 12.3 billion to 8.1 billion for Dickerson, from 9.3 to 5.9 for Tegarden. It's like $3 or $4 billion doesn't mean anything. What is the -- can this be done with any precision or is it more realistic just to say that what the district court would have to do is to figure out whether the State's calculation is within some reasonable range?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Carter G. Phillips",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1287",
      "confidence": 0.93,
      "document_hash": "bc27c1219c7a7059458e5d941385c3e875ad97095f66cd3513c307675135e5fe",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bc27c1219c7a-0000-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 8,
        "start": 8
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "48 of the 50 States have railroads that have to be evaluated and, as the AAR's amicus brief points out, there have been 12 cases on this particular issue."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1287",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "bc27c1219c7a7059458e5d941385c3e875ad97095f66cd3513c307675135e5fe",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bc27c1219c7a-0000-0004",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. HALLWARD-DRIEMEIER: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the court: Congress did not, in the 4-R Act, establish a right to obtain in Federal court administrative review"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1287",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bc27c1219c7a7059458e5d941385c3e875ad97095f66cd3513c307675135e5fe",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bc27c1219c7a-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What if you take my variation on it, and that is, we don't defer it to the State's choice in the abstract, i.e. if we -- if there are 10 methods, as long as it's one of the 10 it's okay? Rather, if we defer at all, we defer it to the choice that is made to -- to accept one or the other methodology, and we look to the reasons that the State gives for making that choice? Would that take all the teeth out of the Federal act?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Hallward-Driemeier",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1287",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bc27c1219c7a7059458e5d941385c3e875ad97095f66cd3513c307675135e5fe",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bc27c1219c7a-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, I think that it would, to a large extent, undermine the right and protection that Congress intended."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1287",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bc27c1219c7a7059458e5d941385c3e875ad97095f66cd3513c307675135e5fe",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bc27c1219c7a-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Your point is well taken. It has a variety, and then it makes some kind of a judgmental choice as between the results it gets. But that I guess just makes my question more complicated. As long as it -- as long as it has good reasons for selecting, let's say the three competing methodologies that it uses, and as long as it has a rational -- gives a rational basis for the ultimate resolution, should we not or should a court not defer to the choices?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Hallward-Driemeier",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1287",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bc27c1219c7a7059458e5d941385c3e875ad97095f66cd3513c307675135e5fe",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bc27c1219c7a-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, I think that to call the weighing of the three evidences of value that might be generated by cost income, sales methods, a choice of methodology to which the Federal court is required to defer is a misnomer, because it really is an exercise of judgment with respect to the facts of the case and the reliability of the data available."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1287",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bc27c1219c7a7059458e5d941385c3e875ad97095f66cd3513c307675135e5fe",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bc27c1219c7a-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Even if you don't agree with Justice Souter's test for the district court review of the State, might you agree with it as applied to the court of appeals review of the district court's job?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Hallward-Driemeier",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1287",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bc27c1219c7a7059458e5d941385c3e875ad97095f66cd3513c307675135e5fe",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bc27c1219c7a-0001-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I do believe that the court of appeals review is reviewing a determination of fact by the district court. There may be subsidiary questions of the law that the court of appeals would review."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1287",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bc27c1219c7a7059458e5d941385c3e875ad97095f66cd3513c307675135e5fe",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bc27c1219c7a-0001-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "May I ask this question? I understood you to be arguing that the district court made an error of law in not receiving evidence of a different methodology offered by the railroads. And also, if they conceivably -- if I understood you correctly, you could send the case back, they could then receive the evidence and come to precisely the same conclusion that they already came to. That would be permissible, wouldn't it?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Hallward-Driemeier",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1287",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bc27c1219c7a7059458e5d941385c3e875ad97095f66cd3513c307675135e5fe",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bc27c1219c7a-0001-0007",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "It would be permissible. You're absolutely right, Justice Stephens."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Calvert",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1287",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bc27c1219c7a7059458e5d941385c3e875ad97095f66cd3513c307675135e5fe",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bc27c1219c7a-0001-0008",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Subsection (b)(1) constitutes a narrow exception to the normal rule of Federal non-interference in State tax administration that is designed to prohibit a specific type of tax discrimination."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1287",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bc27c1219c7a7059458e5d941385c3e875ad97095f66cd3513c307675135e5fe",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bc27c1219c7a-0001-0011",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 34,
        "start": 34
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In other words, there was -- there was a contest about the judgment used by the State in selecting one point of datum in its calculation, right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Calvert",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1287",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bc27c1219c7a7059458e5d941385c3e875ad97095f66cd3513c307675135e5fe",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bc27c1219c7a-0001-0012",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 35,
        "start": 35
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, because it's one thing to say that the State can choose the valuation formula; it's another thing that the State is able to dictate how you fill in the variables."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "CSX",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1287",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bc27c1219c7a7059458e5d941385c3e875ad97095f66cd3513c307675135e5fe",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bc27c1219c7a-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "what CSX asked in this case is that -- they have taken the position, and I believe they still take it for this Court, that a stock-and-debt method will never give a reliable estimate of the value of the railroad. So they want -- what they want to come out of this litigation is the State of Georgia cannot use that at all."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1287",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bc27c1219c7a7059458e5d941385c3e875ad97095f66cd3513c307675135e5fe",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bc27c1219c7a-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "May I ask this question about your position? Supposing Dickerson had adopted the stock-and-debt method and taken the $12 million -- $12 billion figure and said that's really a reasonable basis, and then he ended up with that value. Would you be able to defend that decision with precisely the same arguments that you're making today?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1287",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bc27c1219c7a7059458e5d941385c3e875ad97095f66cd3513c307675135e5fe",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bc27c1219c7a-0002-0002",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "He's done well over a thousand appraisals involving the application of the unit rule."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "CSX",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1287",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bc27c1219c7a7059458e5d941385c3e875ad97095f66cd3513c307675135e5fe",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bc27c1219c7a-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the argument that CSX is making in this case would also put -- put into play a State's most fundamental methodological choice between the unit rule and the summation approaches, and CSX's reply brief admits as such."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1287",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "bc27c1219c7a7059458e5d941385c3e875ad97095f66cd3513c307675135e5fe",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bc27c1219c7a-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "is there any other valuation process that would work this way"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1287",
      "confidence": 0.0,
      "document_hash": "bc27c1219c7a7059458e5d941385c3e875ad97095f66cd3513c307675135e5fe",
